WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:14.560
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And to explain why we're doing this week's show the way that we're doing it on the subject that we're doing it, I'm joined in the studio by one of our producers, Alex Blumberg. Hello, Alex.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和公共广播国际部，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。为了解释我们为什么会以这种方式制作本周的节目，以及为什么要讨论这个主题，演播室里请来了我们的制作人之一，亚历克斯·布伦伯格。你好，亚历克斯。

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.080
<v Alex Blumberg>Hello, Ira.
<v Alex Blumberg>你好，艾拉。

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:16.640
<v Ira Glass>Alex.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯。

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.880
<v Alex Blumberg>Yes.
<v Alex Blumberg>在。

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.240
<v Ira Glass>What's the reason for this week's show?
<v Ira Glass>本周节目的目的是什么？

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:55.240
<v Alex Blumberg>Well, I've been thinking about that, and for me, the reason for today's program goes back to a book I read when I was 15 called The Women's Room. Maybe people listening will remember The Women's Room. It was a sort of a seminal feminist novel from the '70s. And I first came across The Women's Room when I was digging through my parents' bookshelves looking for porn, books with naughty parts in them, like Fear of Flying. And so I picked it up, and I read it.
<v Alex Blumberg>嗯，我一直在思考这个问题。对我来说，今天节目的起因要追溯到我15岁时读的一本书，叫《女性房间》。也许听众们还记得《女性房间》，它是70年代一部具有开创意义的女权主义小说。我第一次接触到这本书是在翻找父母书架寻找色情内容时，也就是那些带有露骨情节的书，比如《飞向恐惧》。于是我拿起它读了起来。

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.140
<v Ira Glass>Because the word "women" was in the title?
<v Ira Glass>是因为书名里有“女性”这个词吗？

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:06.370
<v Alex Blumberg>In lipstick, probably-- or maybe not. Yes.
<v Alex Blumberg>大概是因为封面是唇膏色吧——或许也不是。是的。

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:33.530
<v Alex Blumberg>It's incredibly polemical. It describes basically a group of women going from the '50s through the '60s. And they all suffer at the hands of the various men in their lives. And there's constantly-- women are slowly being driven crazy by their husbands' incessant criticism, or they're being called ugly after they get mastectomies, or they're being stifled by their husbands' emotional shallowness. And--
<v Alex Blumberg>这本书非常有争议。它基本上描述了一群女性从50年代到60年代的经历。她们都在各自生活中的男性手中饱受折磨。书里不断提到——女性因丈夫不断的批评而慢慢变得疯狂，或是在做完乳房切除手术后被说丑陋，又或是因丈夫情感上的浅薄而感到窒息。而且——

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.230
<v Ira Glass>And I noticed you happen to have brought into the studio with you today a copy of The Women's Room.
<v Ira Glass>我注意到你今天正好带了一本《女性房间》到演播室。

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:54.010
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah, well, I recently reread it just to see. For example, I bookmarked a couple of these things. On page 198, the narrator comes in, and she says, "You think I hate men. I guess I do."
<v Alex Blumberg>是的，我最近重读了一遍，看看情况如何。比如，我为其中几处做了书签。在第198页，叙述者写道：“你以为我恨男人。我想我是恨的。”

00:01:54.010 --> 00:02:38.340
<v Alex Blumberg>And then a little bit further down, she says, "My feelings about men are the result of my experience. I have little sympathy for them. Like a Jew just released from Dachau, I watch the handsome young Nazi soldier fall writhing to the ground with a bullet in his stomach, and I look briefly and walk on. I don't even need to shrug. I simply don't care." And then a little bit later, "My hatred is learned from experience. That is not prejudice. I wish it were prejudice. Then, perhaps, I could unlearn it." [CHUCKLES] There's not very much pornographic about that.
<v Alex Blumberg>再往下一点，她说：“我对男人的感受源于我的经历。我一点也不怜悯他们。就像一个刚从达豪集中营释放出来的犹太人，我看着那个英俊的纳粹年轻士兵肚子中弹，在地上痛苦地扭动，我只是看了一眼就走开了。我甚至连耸肩都不需要。我根本不在乎。”稍后又写道：“我的仇恨是从经验中习得的。那不是偏见。我希望那是偏见，那样的话，也许我还能戒掉它。” [笑声] 这其中可没什么色情成分。

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:58.060
<v Alex Blumberg>It's an incredibly strange thing to read this book at that age because at that age, I felt like, there's one other woman who comes into play at this point, and that's-- I probably shouldn't say her name. Let's just call her Kelly. And she was in eighth grade with me, and she sat two rows away from me.
<v Alex Blumberg>在那个年纪读这本书感觉非常奇妙，因为当时我感觉，还有另一个女人出现在我的生活中，那就是——我大概不该说出她的名字。我们暂且叫她凯莉吧。她和我同在八年级，坐在离我两排远的地方。

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:31.140
<v Alex Blumberg>And I remember one time sitting in homeroom with her and seeing her button-down shirt was open a little bit, and I could catch the barest sliver of her little training bra. And it was perhaps the most desire I've ever felt in my life, maybe. I mean, it was just-- it was all-consuming. And I mean, I couldn't concentrate for the rest of the day. I remember it to this day.
<v Alex Blumberg>我记得有一次坐在自习课上，看到她的衬衫扣子开了点，我看到了她训练文胸的一角。那可能是我这辈子感受到的最强烈的欲望，或许吧。我的意思是，那简直是——让人难以自拔。我那天剩下的时间里完全无法集中注意力。直到今天我都记得那一幕。

00:03:31.140 --> 00:04:14.190
<v Alex Blumberg>And so, at the time I was reading this book, I felt like, oh my god, this is what-- I could see myself becoming the people that she was describing, the men she was describing. And it was really terrifying. My testosterone and how it affects me and how I react to it I think about on a daily basis, all the time. It often feels like there's something in my body giving me instructions that I probably shouldn't follow.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，当时我在读那本书时，我心想，天哪，这就是——我能预见自己会变成她笔下的那些人，她笔下的那些男人。这真的很可怕。关于我的睾酮素以及它如何影响我，我如何对它做出反应，我每天都在思考。它总让我觉得身体里有什么东西在给我下指令，而我不该听从。

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:51.190
<v Ira Glass>OK, which brings us to today's program. A while back, Alex tried to convince all of us who work on the radio show that we should do an entire program about testosterone. And I have to say, none of us were really buying it, especially the women, who found the subject dull, even though, as Alex tried to point out to them, women have testosterone, and it affects them pretty much exactly the same way that it affects men. I think all of us were fearing a kind of Men Are from Mars, Women Are from Venus sort of thing, how hormones make men be all aggressive and women all nonaggressive in very stereotyped ways, which all seem kind of reductionistic and dumb-headed.
<v Ira Glass>好的，这引出了今天的节目。不久前，亚历克斯试图说服我们电台的所有人，提议制作一期关于睾酮素的专题节目。我必须说，我们没人买账，尤其是女性同事，她们觉得这个话题很无聊。尽管亚历克斯试图向她们指出，女性体内也有睾酮素，而且它对女性的影响几乎和对男性的影响一模一样。我想我们所有人都担心会演变成那种“男人来自火星，女人来自金星”的套路，即荷尔蒙如何让男人变得攻击性强，让女人变得唯唯诺诺，这种刻板印象看起来既片面又愚蠢。

00:04:51.190 --> 00:05:12.680
<v Ira Glass>And then Alex started reading and calling people. And one of the people that he called was this guy who wrote an article for GQ magazine about what happened to him when, because of medical reasons, he stopped producing testosterone. And for four months, he lived without testosterone before they caught the problem. And the way he described living without testosterone was really incredible.
<v Ira Glass>后来亚历克斯开始阅读相关资料并到处联络。他联系的人中有一个男人，他为《GQ》杂志写过一篇文章，讲述了他因为医疗原因停止产生睾酮素后的经历。在查明问题之前，他有四个月是在没有睾酮素的情况下生活的。他描述那种没有睾酮素的生活方式，真的令人难以置信。

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:44.120
<v Man>Everything that I identify as being me, my ambition, my interest in things, my sense of humor, the inflection in my voice, I mean, the quality of my speech even changed in the time that I was without a lot of the hormone. So, yes, the introduction of testosterone returned everything.
<v Man>所有我认同为“我”的东西，我的野心、我对事物的兴趣、我的幽默感、我说话的语调，我的意思是，在我缺乏大部分荷尔蒙的那段时间里，我说话的特质甚至都变了。所以，是的，睾酮素的引入让一切都回归了。

00:05:44.120 --> 00:06:16.890
<v Man>There were things that I find offensive about my own personality that were disconnected then, and it was nice to be without them-- envy, the desire to judge itself. I approached people with a humility that I had never displayed before.
<v Man>那时候，我个性中一些让我反感的特质消失了，没有它们感觉还挺好的——嫉妒，评判他人的欲望。我以一种前所未有的谦卑态度对待他人。

00:06:16.890 --> 00:07:00.660
<v Man>I grew up in a culture, like all of us, that divides the soul from the body, and that that is your singleness, that is your uniqueness. And then I go through this experience where I have small amounts of a bodily chemical removed and then reintroduced, and it changes everything I know as myself. And it violates the sanctity of that understanding. And that's humbling. And it's terrifying.
<v Man>像我们所有人一样，我在一种将灵魂与肉体分离的文化中长大，认为那是你独特的个体性。然后，我经历了这样一种体验：我身体里少量的化学物质被移除，然后再被重新引入，而这改变了我所认知的一切自我。这违反了那种认知的神圣性。这让人感到卑微，也让人感到恐惧。

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:19.520
<v Ira Glass>I think when it comes to this stuff, most of us do not know what to believe. We're caught between thinking that our hormones and body chemistry can determine so much about our personalities and wanting to believe that they don't. And so, today on our program, we bring you four stories exploring that question-- how much does testosterone determine?
<v Ira Glass>我想在这些问题上，我们大多数人不知道该相信什么。我们在“荷尔蒙和身体化学决定了我们的个性”与“我们不愿相信这一点”之间挣扎。所以，今天在节目中，我们带来四个故事来探讨这个问题——睾酮素到底决定了多少？

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:34.060
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our show, Life at Zero, in which we hear about what it means to lose all your testosterone. Act Two, Infinite Gent-- a woman gets pumped up with several times the testosterone that most men have and describes some surprising changes.
<v Ira Glass>节目的第一幕，生命归零，我们将听到失去所有睾酮素意味着什么。第二幕，无限绅士——一个女性被注入了几倍于常人的睾酮素，并描述了一些令人惊讶的变化。

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:57.710
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Contest-osterone-- in that act, at Alex's suggestion, all of us on the staff of this radio program decide to find out who has the most and who has the least testosterone with lab tests to see how the levels match with our personality traits, an exercise, I have to say, most of us at this point would not recommend that you try at your workplace or with your friends.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，睾酮竞赛——在那一幕中，应亚历克斯的建议，我们节目的全体员工决定通过实验室测试来看看谁的睾酮素最多，谁的最少，以此看看结果是否与我们的性格特征相符。我必须说，这是一个我们目前不建议大家在办公室或朋友间尝试的实验。

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:19.070
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Learning to Shut Up-- in that act, a mother asks her teenage son all her questions about what it means to be a boy, which is, of course, exactly the sort of thing you do not want your mother asking you on tape at 15. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，学会闭嘴——在那一幕中，一位母亲问了她十几岁的儿子所有关于作为男孩意味着什么的问题，这当然是你在15岁时最不希望你妈妈在录音时问你的那种问题。请继续收听。

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:36.950
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Life at Zero. Testosterone is the hormone of desire. And by that, I don't mean sexual desire. I mean desire, period. In this act, we continue with Alex's interview with that magazine writer from GQ who wrote about his experiences with testosterone anonymously.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，生命归零。睾酮素是欲望的荷尔蒙。我指的不是性欲，我指的是欲望本身。在这一幕中，我们继续听亚历克斯对那位匿名写下自己睾酮素经历的《GQ》杂志撰稿人的采访。

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:51.270
<v Man>When you have no testosterone, you have no desire. And when you have no desire, you don't have any content in your mind. You don't think about anything.
<v Man>当你没有睾酮素时，你就没有欲望。而当你没有欲望时，你的大脑里就没有任何内容。你不会去想任何事情。

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:55.390
<v Alex Blumberg>And during those months, how were you behaving? What was different?
<v Alex Blumberg>在那几个月里，你的行为是怎样的？有什么不同？

00:08:55.390 --> 00:09:29.750
<v Man>It wasn't that I was behaving. It was that I was not behaving at all. I was, when I was awake, literally sitting in bed and staring at the wall with neither interest, nor disinterest, for three, four hours at a time. If you'd had a camera in the room, you would have thought I was comatose. I would go out, I would buy some groceries early in the morning, and that would be it. My day had no content. I had no interest in even watching TV, much less reading the newspaper or a book.
<v Man>并不是我在“表现”什么，而是我根本没有任何行为。醒着的时候，我确实就是坐在床上盯着墙看，既没有兴趣，也没有没兴趣，一坐就是三四个小时。如果你房间里有摄像头，你会以为我昏迷了。我会出门，清晨买点杂货，仅此而已。我的一天没有任何内容。我甚至对看电视没有兴趣，更不用说看报纸或书了。

00:09:29.750 --> 00:10:04.440
<v Man>Food-- I didn't want my food to taste good or interesting. And when you're blessed with that lack of desire, you can eat a loaf of Wonder Bread with mayonnaise. And that will be your day. And I only saw my girlfriend on weekends since she was living in New York and I was living in Philly, so I could get away with it five days at a time. And needless to say, there was absolutely no desire.
<v Man>食物——我不想让我的食物变得美味或有趣。当你拥有这种缺乏欲望的福气时，你可以吃一条抹了蛋黄酱的普通白面包，那就会是你的一天。由于我女朋友住在纽约，我住在费城，我只有周末才能见到她，所以我可以连续五天这样混过去。不用说，那时候绝对没有任何欲望。

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:23.640
<v Man>People who are deprived of testosterone don't become Spock-like and incredibly rational. They become nonsensical because they're unable to distinguish between what is and isn't interesting and what is worth noting and what isn't.
<v Man>缺乏睾酮素的人并不会变得像史波克那样极其理性。他们会变得荒谬，因为他们无法区分什么是感兴趣的，什么不是，什么值得注意，什么不值得。

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.440
<v Alex Blumberg>Describe your thoughts on your morning walk in this state.
<v Alex Blumberg>描述一下你在那种状态下早晨散步时的想法。

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:52.610
<v Man>It's very quiet at 5:30, 6:00, 6:30 in the morning. And yeah, and I would see a brick and a wall, and I would think, a brick and a wall. I would see a pigeon and think, a pigeon. It's the most literal possible understanding of the world.
<v Man>早晨5:30、6:00、6:30的时候非常安静。是的，我会看到一块砖和一面墙，然后心想：一块砖和一面墙。我会看到一只鸽子，然后心想：一只鸽子。这是对世界最字面意义上的理解。

00:10:52.610 --> 00:11:01.530
<v Alex Blumberg>So in this time, when you're without testosterone, you're walking down the street. You're just sort of ticking things off, just making these very simple observations--
<v Alex Blumberg>所以在没有睾酮素的这段时间，你走在街上。你只是像在打勾一样，做着非常简单的观察——

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:02.510
<v Man>Like a grocery list.
<v Man>就像一份购物清单。

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:07.830
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah, like a grocery list. Exactly. You also have a thought that comes to you all the time, right?
<v Alex Blumberg>是的，就像一份购物清单。完全正确。你脑子里还有一个总会出现的想法，对吧？

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:07.930
<v Man>Yes.
<v Man>是的。

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.610
<v Alex Blumberg>Talk about that.
<v Alex Blumberg>说说那个。

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:28.810
<v Man>Which is a very strange-sounding thing, which is, that is beautiful. Everything I saw, I thought, that is beautiful, which is odd-sounding, I know, because that sounds like the judgment of a person with passion. But it was the exact opposite.
<v Man>这是一个听起来非常奇怪的想法，那就是：那很美。我看到的一切，我都想：那很美。这听起来很奇怪，我知道，因为这听起来像是充满激情的人的评判。但事实恰恰相反。

00:11:28.810 --> 00:12:01.780
<v Man>It was thought and sometimes even said with complete dispassion, with objectivity. And you see, I was looking at absolutely everything, the most mundane sight in the world-- a weed in the sidewalk-- and thinking, oh, that's beautiful. The surgery scars on people's knees, the bolts in the hubcaps of cars-- all of it. It just seemed to have purpose. And it was like, oh, that's beautiful.
<v Man>那是完全不动感情、客观地思考，甚至有时说出口的。你看，我在观察绝对一切，世界上最平庸的景象——人行道上的一株杂草——然后心想：哦，那很美。人们膝盖上的手术疤痕，汽车轮毂上的螺栓——所有这些。它似乎都有目的。就像是，哦，那很美。

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:30.900
<v Alex Blumberg>It's so staggering that that is the core thought that you were left with. Like, if you see things factually, you could have just as easily settled on monstrous or disgusting. And so it's just-- it's interesting to me that the adjective your brain and what was left of your personality chose to ascribe to everything is "beautiful." Why do you think that is?
<v Alex Blumberg>令人震惊的是，这竟然是你最终留下的核心想法。如果你以事实的角度看事物，你本来可以同样轻易地认为它们是“怪异”或“恶心”的。所以这很有趣，你的大脑和你剩下的个性选择将“美丽”这个形容词赋予了一切。你觉得为什么会这样？

00:12:30.900 --> 00:13:08.870
<v Man>I mean, when I think about that question, the issue of God comes into the equation for me. In a way, being without testosterone brought me closer to God but not in the afternoon-talk-show sense of being-- I don't know-- more humane but actually thinking like God. And of course, I don't mean thinking as God, but I meant thinking like God in an aping, superficial kind of way. He sees things as they really are. He sees you as you really are.
<v Man>我的意思是，当我思考这个问题时，上帝的问题出现在了我的等式中。在某种程度上，没有睾酮素让我更接近上帝，但不是那种下午脱口秀意义上的——我不知道——更有人情味，而是像上帝一样思考。当然，我不是指像上帝那样思考，我是指以一种模仿的、肤浅的方式像上帝一样思考。他看事物本来的样子。他看你本来的样子。

00:13:08.870 --> 00:14:05.470
<v Man>And I had this omniscient sense, when I was without testosterone, that I was seeing through the skin of things, that I was seeing things as they really were, and that the objective conclusion-- not the judgmental one, but the objective conclusion-- was, they are beautiful. Everything is beautiful, from the bugs to the cracks in the sidewalk, to the faces of other people. And it was automatic. Perhaps to see things objectively is to see them, all of them, as beautiful. But in the most-- you have to understand that the thought was expressed in the most flatline, boring way possible. Oh, yeah, that's beautiful. That's beautiful.
<v Man>当我没有睾酮素时，我有一种全知的感觉，仿佛我能透过事物的表象，看到它们的本来面目，而客观的结论——不是主观的评判，而是客观的结论——就是：它们很美。一切都很美，从虫子到人行道上的裂缝，再到他人的脸庞。而且那是自动的。也许客观地看待事物就是看到它们——全部都——是美丽的。但你要明白，这个想法是以最平淡、最无聊的方式表达出来的。哦，是啊，那很美。那很美。

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:43.200
<v Man>You would think that this would be a terrible thing, a terrible state to be in. And for most people, it is. But it was weirdly pleasant. And there is a certain appeal, an impossible appeal, to that Rip Van Winkle existence of being without testosterone. You just have to remember that it doesn't matter if you have nothing if you want nothing. Very tricky to get inside that mindset. In some ways, it's difficult for me to even remember it now, but it had its allure.
