<v Griffin Hansbury>--all that stuff that you're not supposed to do. So I'm still very much learning how to be a man in the world. There's a lot to learn. Men-- walking the street is a constant battle. It's a constant contest. I began to notice, once I started to pass as a man, that single men walking alone down the street will veer away from their path to walk towards me, get in my space, and then veer back. And it's very much like a aggressive move.
<v Griffin Hansbury>——所有那些你不该做的事情。所以我仍然在学习如何在这个世界上作为一个男人生活。有很多东西要学。男人们——走在街上是一场持续的战斗。这是一种持续的竞赛。我开始注意到，一旦我开始被当作男人看待，单独走在街上的单身男性会偏离他们的路径向我走来，进入我的空间，然后再偏离回去。这很像是一种攻击性的行为。

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:34.310
<v Griffin Hansbury>I've had men, just angry guys walking on the street, just body check me. So I really feel like I have to puff myself up so that people will keep their distance a little bit. But if I'm off guard and I'm walking around and I'm enjoying the scenery, it's pretty much guaranteed that somebody will shove me.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我遇到过一些愤怒的家伙走在街上，直接用身体撞我。所以我真的觉得我必须让自己看起来强壮一些，这样人们才会保持点距离。但如果我没防备，四处闲逛享受风景，几乎可以保证有人会推我。

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:37.470
<v Alex Blumberg>Were there specific things that you were hoping for?
<v Alex Blumberg>你当时有没有特别期待的事情？

00:27:37.470 --> 00:28:01.950
<v Griffin Hansbury>I think that the main thing that you hope for, that one hopes for, and that I hoped for when starting testosterone, was to pass as male, to be perceived by the world as a man. But I do have a sort of love-hate relationship with passing that my whole deeper self becomes invisible, and my history becomes invisible. And I think that's hard. It's a hard place to be.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我认为你所希望的，一个人所希望的，以及我在开始使用睾酮素时所希望的，主要就是被当作男性，被世界感知为一个男人。但我对这种“通过”有一种又爱又恨的关系，即我整个更深层的自我变得隐形了，我的历史变得隐形了。我认为这很难。这是一个艰难的处境。

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:03.030
<v Alex Blumberg>Right, right.
<v Alex Blumberg>对，对。

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:26.160
<v Griffin Hansbury>Especially because when I got my first job as a man, they didn't know anything about my past. It was very corporate. So I had to let go of any edgy clothing and facial hair and whatnot that I had before, and I became really boring. I mean, I felt like, wow, people must think I'm really boring. If they only knew that I'm so fascinating.
<v Griffin Hansbury>尤其是当我作为男人得到第一份工作时，他们对我过去一无所知。那是非常企业化的环境。所以我不得不放弃任何前卫的服装、面部毛发之类的我以前拥有的东西，我变得非常无聊。我的意思是，我感觉，哇，人们一定觉得我很无聊。要是他们知道我其实是多么迷人就好了。

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:28.430
<v Griffin Hansbury>[LAUGHTER]
<v Griffin Hansbury>[笑声]

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:39.520
<v Griffin Hansbury>Throughout those almost four years, I had to conceal a lot. I would lie about where I went to college because I went to Bryn Mawr, and I couldn't say I went to Bryn Mawr.
<v Griffin Hansbury>在那近四年里，我不得不隐瞒很多东西。我会谎报我大学去哪里，因为我去了布林茅尔学院，而我不能说我去过布林茅尔。

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:41.340
<v Alex Blumberg>What college did you say you went to?
<v Alex Blumberg>那你说了哪所大学？

00:28:41.340 --> 00:29:08.420
<v Griffin Hansbury>[CHUCKLES] Well, Bryn Mawr is in a bi-college relationship with Haverford College. And so I would say I went to Haverford, which is kind of-- I don't want to say anything bad about Haverford. Some of my best friends went there. But I think Bryn Mawr is a superior school to Haverford. I do. And I think it's a superior education. And when I have to say I went to Haverford, it's like a little knife in my heart.
<v Griffin Hansbury>[笑声] 嗯，布林茅尔学院和哈弗福德学院有双校关系。所以我常说我去了哈弗福德，这有点——我不想对哈弗福德说任何坏话。我最好的朋友有一些去了那里。但我认为布林茅尔是比哈弗福德更好的学校。我确实这么认为。我认为那是一种更优越的教育。当我不得不说我去了哈弗福德时，那就像我心里插了一把小刀。

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:09.200
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:15.480
<v Griffin Hansbury>But [CHUCKLES] no offense to my Haverford friends.
<v Griffin Hansbury>但是 [笑声] 对我的哈弗福德朋友们无意冒犯。

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:20.360
<v Alex Blumberg>What do you feel like the biggest thing that you do miss is?
<v Alex Blumberg>你觉得你最怀念的是什么？

00:29:20.360 --> 00:30:01.120
<v Griffin Hansbury>Hmm. Maybe the close relationships that I had with women. I still have close relationships with the women I've known since before T, but I don't make close relationships with new female friends. It's hard. It's hard to do. There's a barrier. So I kind of miss being part of a cool bunch of women. I actually like women better than men. It just so happens that I fit in more as a man. But I think women are really cool. Sisterhood is powerful-- all that stuff, so--
<v Griffin Hansbury>嗯。也许是我和女性之间曾经拥有的亲密关系。我和在T之前就认识的女性仍然保持着亲密关系，但我不会和新的女性朋友建立亲密关系。这很难。很难做到。有一道屏障。所以我有点怀念曾经作为一群很酷的女性中的一员。其实我比男人更喜欢女人。只是刚好我作为男人更合群。但我认为女性真的很酷。姐妹情谊是强大的——所有那些，所以——

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:06.920
<v Alex Blumberg>[CHUCKLES] Yeah, yeah. And what do you think is the biggest thing you've gained?
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声] 是的，是的。你觉得你获得的最大收获是什么？

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:30.530
<v Griffin Hansbury>Biggest thing I've gained-- it's just so great when people call me sir, even after seven years. I don't always hear it, but it still rings a little bell inside my heart, like, oh, sir. It's wonderful. And it's something that when I was a little kid, I used to wish for. And turn me into a boy-- Pinocchio.
<v Griffin Hansbury>获得的最大收获——当人们称呼我为“先生”时，感觉真是太棒了，即使是七年后。我并不总能听到，但它仍然会在我心中敲响一个小钟，就像，哦，先生。那真是太美妙了。这是我小时候曾祈祷过的事情。把我变成男孩——匹诺曹。

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:56.090
<v Griffin Hansbury>And you never really think it's possible. But it is possible. And the other nice thing about it is knowing that that's possible, that you can actually make this enormous leap, means that, well, then anything else must be possible, too, because this is certainly unbelievable.
<v Griffin Hansbury>你从没真的认为那是可能的。但它是可能的。而且关于这一点的另一件好事是，知道了这是可能的，知道了你可以做出这个巨大的飞跃，意味着，嗯，那么其他任何事情也一定是可能的，因为这无疑是令人难以置信的。

00:30:56.090 --> 00:31:06.690
<v Ira Glass>Griffin Hansbury lives in New York. He talked with our producer, Alex Blumberg. Griffin is near completion on a memoir about his own experience transitioning from female to male. He's looking for a publisher.
<v Ira Glass>格里芬·汉斯伯里住在纽约。他与我们的制作人亚历克斯·布伦伯格进行了交流。格里芬正在完成一本关于他自己从女性转变为男性的经历的回忆录。他正在寻找出版商。

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:14.620
<v Alex Blumberg>What occurs to me is that you're in a perfect position to offer romantic advice to anybody who needs it.
<v Alex Blumberg>我想说的是，你处于一个完美的地位，可以为任何需要的人提供浪漫建议。

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:25.100
<v Griffin Hansbury>I know, I know. And I have a friend with a website, and we've been dying to do an online advice column called "Ask a Guy Who Used to Be a Girl."
<v Griffin Hansbury>我知道，我知道。我有一个朋友有个网站，我们一直渴望开一个在线咨询专栏，叫“问一个曾经是女孩的男人”。

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:26.820
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:29.220
<v Griffin Hansbury>We haven't gotten it off the ground yet.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我们还没把它搞起来。

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:37.140
<v Alex Blumberg>You would make a million dollars.
<v Alex Blumberg>你会赚到一百万。

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:56.380
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, who has the most testosterone-- the slightly femme-y host of a public radio program, his staff, or a gay man in New York City? Answer in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，谁拥有最多的睾酮素——是一个公共广播节目的稍显女性化的主持人，他的员工，还是纽约市的一个同性恋男人？一分钟后，当我们的节目继续时，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际部为您揭晓答案。

00:31:56.380 --> 00:32:15.830
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, testosterone, and just how much does it determine of our personalities and fates? Well, we've arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, Contest-osterone.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的各种不同的故事。今天的节目是关于睾酮素的，它对我们的个性和命运到底决定了多少？好吧，我们已经到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，睾酮竞赛。

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:40.470
<v Ira Glass>At some point in putting together this week's radio show, Alex, who you heard a lot of in the first half of our program, suggested that it might be a fun thing to do and might illustrate some interesting principles about testosterone if everybody on the radio staff would get their testosterone level checked. We could each predict who would come out as number one, number two, and so forth, based on their personalities. And then we would compare the results with what each of us had predicted.
<v Ira Glass>在制作本周广播节目的某个时候，你在前半部分节目中听过很多的亚历克斯，建议说，如果电台的每个人都去检查他们的睾酮素水平，那可能会是一件有趣的事情，而且可能阐明一些关于睾酮素的有趣原则。我们可以每个人预测谁会是第一名，谁是第二名等等，基于他们的个性。然后我们将结果与我们每个人的预测进行比较。

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:53.590
<v Ira Glass>Since men always have a lot more testosterone than women, we would do two rankings-- one for the men, one for the women. We invited two of our regular contributors, David Rakoff and Sarah Vowell, to take part with us, making it five men and four women in all.
<v Ira Glass>由于男性体内的睾酮素总是比女性多得多，我们将进行两次排名——男性一次，女性一次。我们邀请了我们两位常驻撰稿人大卫·拉科夫和萨拉·沃威尔参与其中，总共五名男性和四名女性。

00:32:53.590 --> 00:33:16.560
<v Ira Glass>It turns out to be remarkably easy to get your testosterone assessed. You spit into a little tube twice a day and send it to a lab-- in our case, the Yerkes National Primate Research Center. And I think it was Jonathan and Starlee, producers on our show who you sometimes hear during stories on the air, who pointed out first that the entire enterprise might not be such a good idea.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，获得睾酮素评估非常容易。每天吐两次唾液进小管子，然后寄给实验室——在我们的案例中是耶基斯国家灵长类动物研究中心。我认为是乔纳森和斯塔莉，我们节目的制作人，你有时会在节目故事中听到他们，他们首先指出，整个企业可能不是一个好主意。

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:21.960
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I just think we're opening a whole can of worms here that we don't want to be doing.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我只是觉得我们在这里开启了一个我们不想招惹的麻烦。

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:25.960
<v Starlee Kine>It's all very-- well, this whole thing's kind of a mess, diplomatic-wise.
<v Starlee Kine>这一切都很——好吧，从外交角度看，这一切都是一团糟。

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:26.660
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么说？

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:46.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Like, I mean, either we'll walk away from this finding out that Starlee has more testosterone than I do, and she'll end up being incredibly upset, and I'll be upset, and everyone will just be upset. I mean, what do we hope to gain, really, from all of this? This whole thing is going to lead to heartbreak, somehow.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我的意思是，要么我们会发现斯塔莉的睾酮素比我的多，她会因此变得极其沮丧，我也会沮丧，每个人都会很沮丧。我的意思是，从这一切中我们到底能得到什么？这一切最终都会导致心碎，以某种方式。

00:33:46.760 --> 00:33:53.020
<v Wendy Dorr>I've been thinking a lot about it, and I can't really decide whether I want to have more or if I want to have less.
<v Wendy Dorr>我一直在思考它，我真的无法决定我是想拥有更多还是更少。

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.960
<v Ira Glass>This is Wendy, another producer.
<v Ira Glass>这是温迪，另一位制作人。

00:33:54.960 --> 00:34:00.840
<v Wendy Dorr>I don't know. Having more makes me seem like an [BLEEP]-hole.
<v Wendy Dorr>我不知道。拥有更多让我看起来像个混蛋。

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:02.600
<v Wendy Dorr>[LAUGHTER]
<v Wendy Dorr>[笑声]

00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:07.720
<v Wendy Dorr>And having not enough makes me seem like a pushover, sucker.
<v Wendy Dorr>而拥有不够多让我看起来像个好欺负的软蛋。

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:10.040
<v Julie Snyder>For the men, I think it'll be a big deal.
<v Julie Snyder>对于男人来说，我觉得这会是一件大事。

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:12.600
<v Ira Glass>This is Julie, the senior producer of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这是朱莉，我们节目的高级制作人。

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:21.480
<v Julie Snyder>I think it'll change all your interactions with each other. You'll know, when you're talking to each other, who beat you. I think it'll change everything.
<v Julie Snyder>我认为这会改变你们所有人之间的互动。当你们彼此交谈时，你会知道谁赢了你。我觉得这会改变一切。

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:38.840
<v Ira Glass>Nonetheless, we marched toward the abyss. To find out what personality traits actually correlate with high testosterone and low testosterone, we checked with somebody who's done a lot of research on the subject, James Dabbs of Georgia State University, author of a book called Heroes, Rogues, and Lovers-- Testosterone and Behavior.
<v Ira Glass>尽管如此，我们还是走向了深渊。为了找出哪些个性特征真正与高睾酮素和低睾酮素相关，我们咨询了一位在该领域做过大量研究的人——乔治亚州立大学的詹姆斯·达布斯，他是《英雄、流氓和情人——睾酮素与行为》一书的作者。

00:34:38.840 --> 00:35:03.180
<v James Dabbs>The most simple thing, I would say, is it leads to a certain boldness and fearlessness and confidence in a person's behavior. They tend to focus upon what they do, move forward with less conflict. The downside is they don't pay attention to things going on around them, so they get blindsided by accidents and other things going on.
<v James Dabbs>最简单的话来说，它导致了某种大胆、无畏和自信的行为。他们倾向于专注于他们所做的事情，向前迈进，冲突较少。缺点是他们不注意周围发生的事情，所以会被意外和其他发生的事情弄得措手不及。

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:05.050
<v Ira Glass>What are some of the misconceptions you run into?
<v Ira Glass>你遇到过哪些误解？

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:21.210
<v James Dabbs>Well, they think it makes them manly and heroic and virile and sexual, which is not really true. It doesn't take much testosterone to have sex. So I mean, that's beside the point.
<v James Dabbs>嗯，他们认为这使他们变得男人、英雄、阳刚和性欲强，这并不是真的。拥有性并不需要多少睾酮素。所以，我的意思是，那离题了。

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:35.210
<v Ira Glass>What scientific studies can tell us is that, in general, more testosterone does not mean you're a more successful person. In fact, blue-collar workers tend to have more testosterone than white-collar workers, and unemployed men have more than either group.
<v Ira Glass>科学研究告诉我们的是，通常情况下，睾酮素多并不意味着你是一个更成功的人。事实上，蓝领工人的睾酮素往往比白领工人多，而失业男性的睾酮素比这两组人都要多。

00:35:35.210 --> 00:35:59.130
<v Ira Glass>Actors and trial lawyers-- people who basically go on stage, in one way or another-- tend to have very high testosterone levels. Higher testosterone correlates with baldness and with bigger muscles. And researchers say that people with lots of testosterone have a wolfish smile. And people with less testosterone have a kinder smile, and they smile more often, as if to say, don't hurt me.
<v Ira Glass>演员和出庭律师——那些基本上以某种方式登上舞台的人——往往有非常高的睾酮素水平。高睾酮素与秃顶和更大的肌肉有关。研究人员说，睾酮素水平高的人有一种狼性的微笑。而睾酮素水平低的人有一种更友善的微笑，他们笑得更频繁，仿佛在说：别伤害我。

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:12.500
<v Ira Glass>Testosterone goes up when you score a victory at something. It goes down when you suffer a defeat. In one study of World Cup soccer fans watching a match on television, testosterone dropped in fans of the losing team and rose in the fans of the winning team.
<v Ira Glass>当你在一件事上取得胜利时，睾酮素水平会上升。当你遭受失败时，它会下降。在一项针对在电视上观看比赛的世界杯足球迷的研究中，失败方的球迷睾酮素水平下降，而获胜方的球迷睾酮素水平上升。

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:20.780
<v James Dabbs>For people, there's not a lot of difference between being in the contest and watching the contest. We can empathize with it so well.
<v James Dabbs>对于人类来说，参与竞赛和观看竞赛之间没有太大的区别。我们能很好地对它产生共鸣。

00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:33.580
<v Ira Glass>So what would all this mean for our radio staff? Well, when it came to predicting which of the women would have the highest testosterone, there was a clear majority in the voting. Here are Sarah Vowell, Todd, our production manager, and Wendy weighing in.
<v Ira Glass>那么这对我们的电台员工意味着什么呢？好吧，当预测哪个女性会有最高的睾酮素时，投票中出现了绝对多数。这是萨拉·沃威尔，我们的生产经理托德，以及温迪的看法。

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:35.020
<v Sarah Vowell>Julie will win.
<v Sarah Vowell>朱莉会赢。

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:39.300
<v Todd Bachmann>Julie's tough. She can certainly push me around sometimes.
<v Todd Bachmann>朱莉很硬。她有时候确实能对我推三阻四。

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:48.140
<v Wendy Dorr>I think Julie has the most because she is the boldest of all of us, right? She says the boldest things.
<v Wendy Dorr>我觉得朱莉最高，因为她是我们在座中最胆大的，对吧？她说的最胆大。

00:36:48.140 --> 00:37:03.750
<v Ira Glass>When we're all together in the same place, it is clearly Julie who is the alpha of the whole group. Though, at some point in putting together this week's radio show, Alex and Julie sat down in a studio, and he asked her to predict which women would have the highest testosterone. And here's how she lined it up.
<v Ira Glass>当我们都在同一个地方时，显然朱莉是整个群体的头领。虽然，在制作本周广播节目的某个时候，亚历克斯和朱莉坐在演播室里，他让她预测哪个女性会有最高的睾酮素。这是她安排的顺序。

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:12.430
<v Julie Snyder>Wendy first, and then Starlee, and then Sarah, I guess.
<v Julie Snyder>温迪第一，然后是斯塔莉，然后是萨拉，我想。

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:12.970
<v Alex Blumberg>And then?
<v Alex Blumberg>然后呢？

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:16.250
<v Julie Snyder>And then me, just passive me--
<v Julie Snyder>然后是我，仅仅是消极的我——

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:17.450
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah.
<v Alex Blumberg>是的。

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:21.150
<v Julie Snyder>--just taking it easy. Girly, girly me--
<v Julie Snyder>——只是放轻松。娘娘腔的我——

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:21.830
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHS]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:26.690
<v Julie Snyder>--just being feminine over here in the corner. Honestly, who do I think?
<v Julie Snyder>——只是作为一个女性在角落里。老实说，我觉得谁最高？

00:37:26.690 --> 00:37:28.510
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah.
<v Alex Blumberg>是的。

00:37:28.510 --> 00:37:30.830
<v Julie Snyder>Well, I fear me.
<v Julie Snyder>嗯，我怕我自己。

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:32.530
