<v Miriam Toews>欧文从小就是一个安静的孩子——不是阴郁，而是相当沉默。当他还是婴儿时，我推着他的婴儿车，人们会停下来蹲下和他说话，指望他笑。他只会叹气，盯着他们看。

00:46:38.220 --> 00:47:05.110
<v Miriam Toews>When he got a little older and went over to his friends' places to play and it was time for him to go home, he just got up and walked right out the door to the car. He didn't say, hey, thanks for having me, or next time, we'll play at my place, or even goodbye. We'd be driving home, and I'd be trying to explain to him that it seemed rude, and that sometimes, we just had to say certain things at certain times. It was polite and expected. He'd just sigh and nod and kick the dash or stick his head entirely out the window.
<v Miriam Toews>当他大一点，去朋友家玩，该回家的时候，他只是站起来就直接走出门上车。他不说什么“嘿，谢谢你们邀请我”或“下次来我家玩”或甚至“再见”。我们在开车回家的路上，我试图向他解释这似乎很粗鲁，有时我们必须在特定时间说特定的话。这是礼貌且被期待的。他只会叹气、点头、踢仪表板，或把头完全伸出窗外。

00:47:05.110 --> 00:47:23.590
<v Miriam Toews>But back then, I could figure out what was going on in his head, even if he didn't come right out and say it, like when he was three, and he didn't want me to go out. Instead of grabbing my leg or having a tantrum or something, he'd hide my shoes and then sit on the couch, pretending to read the newspaper, while I searched all over the place.
<v Miriam Toews>但那时，我能猜出他脑子里在想什么，即使他没有直接说出来，比如当他三岁时，他不希望我出去。他不是抱住我的腿或发脾气之类的，他会把我的鞋藏起来，然后坐在沙发上，假装看报纸，而我到处找。

00:47:23.590 --> 00:47:55.840
<v Miriam Toews>When he was eight and angry and sitting in a tree and throwing stones at the car or something, I'd go outside, knowing it would be a matter of minutes before he'd start to cry and then slowly spill his guts about whatever series of events it was that led to his meltdown and that the whole scene would end with hugs and apologies and probably a large muscle activity, like him cheerfully taking shots at my head with a soccer ball. Now, though, the signs are harder, if not impossible, to read. I'm not sure there are any, actually. And I have no idea where to begin my search.
<v Miriam Toews>当他八岁，愤怒地坐在树上，往车上扔石头什么的，我会走出去，知道只需几分钟，他就会开始哭，然后慢慢把憋在心里的事倾倒出来，关于导致他崩溃的一系列事件，整个场景会以拥抱和道歉结束，可能还会有一项大型肌肉活动，比如他愉快地用足球向我的头射门。但现在，这些迹象更难读懂了，如果不是不可能的话。我不确定实际上还有没有迹象。我也不知道从哪里开始搜索。

00:47:55.840 --> 00:48:00.760
<v Miriam Toews>What do you think girls think about you when they think about you?
<v Miriam Toews>你觉得女孩们想到你时，会怎么想？

00:48:00.760 --> 00:48:04.240
<v Owen Toews>I don't know. Nothing.
<v Owen Toews>不知道。什么都不会想。

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:07.920
<v Miriam Toews>You think no girl ever thinks anything about you?
<v Miriam Toews>你觉得从没女孩对你有任何想法吗？

00:48:07.920 --> 00:48:13.040
<v Owen Toews>Maybe. I don't know.
<v Owen Toews>也许吧。不知道。

00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:36.680
<v Miriam Toews>He brought 85 CDs with him for a family road trip that would last 11 days. He also brought his sketchbook, which started out as an art class project and has since evolved into a kind of diary, I think. He tends to write things down like, "Jesus, I got to find someone irrational," or, "The [BLEEP] I'm passing off as writing is slowly killing me."
<v Miriam Toews>他为一次长达11天的家庭公路旅行带了85张CD。他还带了他的素描本，那最初是一个美术课作业，后来演变成了一种日记，我想。他倾向于写下诸如：“天哪，我得找个非理性的人，”或，“我当作写作的垃圾正在慢慢杀死我。”

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:58.450
<v Miriam Toews>The first day of our road trip through North Dakota to South, the music war started. His 12-year-old sister, Georgia, claimed the very back seat of the minivan and was content to lie there, surrounded by nail polish and beads and thread and Hershey Kisses and Archie comics, and listen to her music on her Discman, mostly R&B stuff, Destiny's Child and the Save the Last Dance soundtrack.
<v Miriam Toews>我们穿越北达科他州到南达科他州的公路旅行的第一天，音乐战争就开始了。他12岁的妹妹乔治亚占领了小型货车的最后一排，满足地躺在那里，被指甲油、珠子、线、好时之吻巧克力和阿奇漫画包围，用她的随身听听音乐，大部分是R&B，比如真命天女和《留住最后一支舞》的原声带。

00:48:58.450 --> 00:49:14.490
<v Miriam Toews>My husband, Cassidy, and I sat in the front, and Owen sat on the seat right behind us so he could listen to his CDs on the stereo. We tell him to listen to them on his Discman, which he did sometimes, but then he'd miss out on our sporadic conversations, which he enjoyed either participating in or mocking.
<v Miriam Toews>我丈夫卡西迪和我坐在前排，欧文坐在我们身后的座位上，这样他就可以在音响上听他的CD。我们告诉他用随身听听，他有时会照做，但那样他就会错过我们偶尔的对话，他要么喜欢参与，要么喜欢嘲弄。

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:38.890
<v Miriam Toews>Sometimes he'd give us a choice of his CDs to play. Nirvana, he'd say. You guys like Nirvana, right? Yeah, but the unplugged one, we'd say. Oh, Lord, help me now, he'd say. He'd pop a dozen CDs into the player-- They Will Know Us by the Trail of Dead, Black Rebel Motorcycle Club, At the Drive-In, other stuff. And we'd listen to half a song from each one and go, nope, nope, nope.
<v Miriam Toews>有时他会给我们选几张他的CD放。“涅槃乐队，”他说。“你们喜欢涅槃，对吧？”“是的，但不插电的那张，”我们说。“哦，主啊，帮帮我吧，”他说。他会在播放器里塞进十几张CD——比如《他们会通过死人之踪认出我们》、《黑色叛逆摩托车俱乐部》、《在驾驶座上》，以及其他东西。我们每张听半首歌，然后说：不，不，不。

00:49:38.890 --> 00:50:01.610
<v Miriam Toews>I had wanted to learn more about him from his music. I was pretty sure his lyrics would tell me more about him than he would. But the problem was I couldn't understand most of the lyrics, and none of his CDs, which he'd bootlegged, had liner notes. We asked him if he didn't miss having liner notes, and he said, man, liner notes. You guys have this unnatural relationship with your liner notes.
<v Miriam Toews>我曾想通过他的音乐更多地了解他。我敢肯定他的歌词会比他本人告诉我更多关于他的事。但问题是我听不懂大部分歌词，他盗版的CD也没歌词本。我们问他是否不怀念歌词本，他说：“天哪，歌词本。你们对你们的歌词本有一种不自然的关系。”

00:50:01.610 --> 00:50:04.560
<v Miriam Toews>What are some of your favorite lyrics?
<v Miriam Toews>你最喜欢的一些歌词是什么？

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:11.090
<v Owen Toews>Mm. I like some of the lyrics from the Pixies.
<v Owen Toews>嗯。我喜欢小精灵乐队的一些歌词。

00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:16.850
<v Miriam Toews>Can you say some of the lyrics from the Pixies?
<v Miriam Toews>你能说出一些小精灵乐队的歌词吗？

00:50:16.850 --> 00:50:20.210
<v Owen Toews>"You're so pretty when you're unfaithful to me."
<v Owen Toews>“当你对我背叛时，你真漂亮。”

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:23.530
<v Miriam Toews>So, what does that mean to you?
<v Miriam Toews>那么，这对你意味着什么？

00:50:23.530 --> 00:50:27.930
<v Owen Toews>I don't know. I just thought it was funny.
<v Owen Toews>不知道。我只是觉得它很有趣。

00:50:27.930 --> 00:51:02.220
<v Miriam Toews>When Owen was little, I discovered I could get him to do anything by timing him. Go get your jacket, I'd say, or, clean up your toys, quick. I'll time you. He loved competing with himself and beating his old records, even if I just made up times. And it evolved into a huge, competitive, sports-loving thing that I still don't really understand or enjoy. That timing thing didn't work at all with Georgia. She'd just look up at me and say, what do you mean, time me? What for?
<v Miriam Toews>当欧文小的时候，我发现可以通过给他计时来让他做任何事。去拿你的夹克，我说，或者，快点把你的玩具收拾干净。我会为你计时。他喜欢和自己竞争，打破旧纪录，即使我只是胡乱编造时间。这演变成了一个巨大的、竞争性的、热爱运动的事情，我到现在还不真正理解或享受。那种计时方法在乔治亚身上一点都不管用。她只会抬头看着我说：你是什么意思，计时？为了什么？

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:25.820
<v Miriam Toews>The difference between him and Georgia in the talking department is huge. Georgia will come home from school and literally reenact her entire day so that she's telling me about it almost in real time, hour after hour. Owen, on the other hand, might tell me that his day was good, even if it was bad, but usually, he just shrugs and makes an "I don't know" kind of sound. And then he'll head down to the basement and play his guitar.
<v Miriam Toews>他在说话方面与乔治亚的差距巨大。乔治亚会放学回家，真的重演她的一整天，以至于她几乎是实时地、一小时一小时地告诉我。另一方面，欧文可能会告诉我他这一天过得不错，即使其实很糟糕，但通常他只是耸耸肩，发出一种“我不知道”的声音。然后他会去地下室弹吉他。

00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:31.080
<v Miriam Toews>So some of your friends have girlfriends. And do you ever think that you'd like to have a girlfriend?
<v Miriam Toews>所以你的一些朋友有女朋友。你有没有想过也想有个女朋友？

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:34.060
<v Owen Toews>[CHUCKLES] Yeah.
<v Owen Toews>[笑声] 想过。

00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:43.180
<v Miriam Toews>What do you think they think? How would they describe you to their friends, do you think?
<v Miriam Toews>你觉得她们怎么想？你觉得她们会怎么向朋友形容你？

00:51:43.180 --> 00:51:50.710
<v Owen Toews>Mm. I don't know.
<v Owen Toews>嗯。不知道。

00:51:50.710 --> 00:51:53.630
<v Miriam Toews>Do you think you do know, but you just don't really want to talk about it with me?
<v Miriam Toews>你觉得你知道，但只是不想和我谈论它？

00:51:53.630 --> 00:51:56.350
<v Owen Toews>I think that-- yeah, I think so.
<v Owen Toews>我觉得——是的，我想是的。

00:51:56.350 --> 00:52:04.870
<v Miriam Toews>I can dig it.
<v Miriam Toews>我明白了。

00:52:04.870 --> 00:52:18.550
<v Ira Glass>I've learned over time not to worry too much about his silence, although it used to drive me crazy. I wondered if maybe he'd turn out to be a psychopath, although I'm not sure why I associate evil with silence. Anyway, I'm getting better with it.
<v Ira Glass>随着时间的推移，我已经学会了不要太担心他的沉默，尽管那曾经让我发疯。我怀疑他是否会成为一个心理变态，尽管我不确定为什么要把邪恶与沉默联系起来。无论如何，我对此处理得更好了。

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:38.920
<v Ira Glass>Cassidy often reassures me by saying stuff like, it's a guy thing, or, he's a 15-year-old boy. What do you expect? He told me that when he was Owen's age, he sat in his bedroom by himself for hours on end, typing page after page of angry vitriol against the world on an old manual typewriter until his fingers bled. And he seems really happy now.
<v Ira Glass>卡西迪常安慰我，说些像“这是男孩的事，”或“他是个15岁的男孩。你还指望什么？”之类的话。他告诉我，他像欧文那么大时，曾在卧室里独自坐几个小时，用旧手动打字机打出一页又一页对世界的愤怒抨击，直到手指流血。他现在看起来真的很幸福。

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:57.680
<v Ira Glass>I realize my kids sound like boy and girl stereotypes. Owen is taciturn and into sports, and Georgia is chatty and prone to the occasional crying jag. But it really just is that way. I don't know why. It's not like we planned it. I don't want to think it's because he's a boy and she's a girl. But if it is, who cares?
<v Ira Glass>我意识到我的孩子听起来像男孩和女孩的刻板印象。欧文沉默寡言，喜欢运动，而乔治亚健谈，偶尔会大哭一场。但事实确实如此。我不知道为什么。这不像我们计划好的。我不愿认为这是因为他是男孩，她是女孩。但如果是，谁在乎呢？

00:52:57.680 --> 00:53:19.320
<v Ira Glass>When Georgia was upset about something at school the other day and face down on the couch, he sat on the floor beside her and said, it's hard being a girl, isn't it? I'm not sure that it's any easier being a boy. And I don't know what he does exactly when he's sad, other than listening to music. Except for once, it's been years since I've seen him cry. I'd kill to read his notebook.
<v Ira Glass>前几天乔治亚在学校因为某事感到不安，脸朝下趴在沙发上，他坐在她身边的地板上说：做女孩很难，对吧？我不确定做男孩会更容易。除了听音乐，我不知道他难过时具体做什么。除了一次，我已经好几年没见他哭了。我愿意杀人来读他的笔记。

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:22.560
<v Miriam Toews>Is there anything about girls that you envy?
<v Miriam Toews>关于女孩，有什么是你羡慕的吗？

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:25.750
<v Owen Toews>Mm. No, nothing.
<v Owen Toews>嗯。没有，什么都没有。

00:53:25.750 --> 00:53:25.970
<v Miriam Toews>Nothing?
<v Miriam Toews>什么都没有？

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:26.200
<v Owen Toews>Nothing.
<v Owen Toews>什么都没有。

00:53:26.200 --> 00:53:36.570
<v Miriam Toews>Well, what about their-- it seems-- their ability to talk about how they feel about things easier?
<v Miriam Toews>嗯，关于她们的——看起来——她们能更容易地谈论对事物的感受？

00:53:36.570 --> 00:53:38.690
<v Owen Toews>Sure, yeah.
<v Owen Toews>当然，是的。

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:40.330
<v Miriam Toews>You envy that?
<v Miriam Toews>你羡慕那个？

00:53:40.330 --> 00:53:47.290
<v Owen Toews>I definitely envy that.
<v Owen Toews>我绝对羡慕那个。

00:53:47.290 --> 00:54:19.130
<v Miriam Toews>We were all pretty quiet as we drove up to Colorado Springs on our way to Denver. The sun was shining, but it was raining a bit. And we saw not one, not two, but three dead cows in fields, stiff, with their legs in the air, and it bothered us. After the third dead cow, I played my favorite song of the summer, Gillian Welch's "Elvis Presley Blues." It's a wistful song about the King as a 15-year-old boy, just starting out with a big rock and roll dream in a shirt his mother made. And it was kind of miraculous because Owen actually liked it, too.
<v Miriam Toews>我们在开车去科罗拉多斯普林斯前往丹佛的路上时，大家都很安静。阳光明媚，但下了一点雨。我们没看到一只，没两只，而是三只死牛躺在田里，僵硬着，腿朝天，这让我们很困扰。看到第三只死牛后，我放了我那个夏天最喜欢的歌，吉莉安·韦尔奇的《猫王蓝调》。那是一首关于国王15岁少年时的忧郁歌曲，刚开始有一个巨大的摇滚梦，穿着他妈妈做的衬衫。这简直是个奇迹，因为欧文竟然也喜欢它。

00:54:19.130 --> 00:54:44.460
<v Miriam Toews>For me, it was a song about a boy becoming a man and leaving home, and it made me a little sad. I think Owen was probably hearing it as a song about a cool 15-year-old who was about to conquer the world. But really, I haven't got a clue how he heard the song or how it made him feel. All he said was that it had a decent pop sensibility. And then he was quiet.
<v Miriam Toews>对我来说，这是一首关于男孩成为男人并离开家的歌，它让我有点难过。我认为欧文可能把它当作一首关于一个酷酷的、即将征服世界的15岁少年的歌。但老实说，我根本不知道他是怎么听这首歌的，或者它让他有什么感觉。他只说这首歌有不错的流行感。然后他就安静了。

00:54:44.460 --> 00:55:00.540
<v Miriam Toews>These days, the more I press him for information, the faster he shuts right down. The other day, I tried a new approach-- his. I was annoyed by a bunch of things, and rather than broadcast it all over the house, I sat on the couch and glared at the TV and sighed periodically.
<v Miriam Toews>这些天，我越是逼他提供信息，他关掉的速度就越快。前几天，我尝试了一种新方法——他的方法。我被很多事情弄得很烦，与其在家里大肆宣扬，我坐在沙发上盯着电视，偶尔叹叹气。

00:55:00.540 --> 00:55:36.700
<v Miriam Toews>And strangely, Owen responded. He observed my weirdness for a while and then left. Then he came back and looked at me again and said one word, "Mom," but in such a nice way that I smiled and said, "Owen." And that was it. And then we sat there for a while, silently. I was surprised by how completely comfortable it felt. And then, finally, wordlessly, he got up and took his laundry upstairs.
<v Miriam Toews>奇怪的是，欧文回应了。他观察了一会儿我的古怪，然后离开了。然后他又回来看了我一眼，只说了一个词：“妈妈”，但以一种如此友好的方式，我笑了笑说：“欧文。”就是这样。然后我们坐在那里待了一会儿，沉默着。我惊讶地发现这种感觉是多么完全的舒适。最后，他一言不发地起身，把他的洗衣服拿到了楼上。

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:43.660
<v Ira Glass>Miriam Toews is a writer in Winnipeg. She wrote The X Letters in one of our programs. Her latest book is called Swing Low.
<v Ira Glass>米里亚姆·托斯是温尼伯的一位作家。她在我们的节目中写过《X信件》。她最新的书叫《轻摇》。

00:55:43.660 --> 00:56:07.220
<v Ira Glass>["ALMOST GROWN" BY CHUCK BERRY]
<v Ira Glass>["Almost Grown", 查克·贝瑞演唱]

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:29.060
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself with Wendy Dorr, Jonathan Goldstein, and Starlee Kine, senior producer Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Chris Neary. Special thanks today to El Jefe, to [? Buzzy ?] [? Goldstein, ?] to Robert Powell, to Jim Nelson at GQ, our Daniel Foster, Sarah Vowell, and David Rakoff, who I swear we will never mess with again.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们今天的节目由亚历克斯·布伦伯格和我本人制作，温迪·多尔、乔纳森·戈德斯坦和斯塔莉·凯恩参与，高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。生产帮助来自托德·巴赫曼和克里斯·尼瑞。特别感谢今天的老板，给[？巴兹？][？戈尔茨坦，？]给罗伯特·鲍威尔，给《GQ》的吉姆·纳尔逊，我们的丹尼尔·福斯特、萨拉·沃威尔和大卫·拉科夫，我发誓我们再也不会惹他们了。

00:56:29.060 --> 00:56:59.110
<v Ira Glass>To buy a cassette of this or any of our programs, call us here at WBEZ in Chicago, 312-832-3380, or visit our website, where you can also listen to our programs for free, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life comes to you from the city of Chicago, Richard M. Daley, mayor. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. Funding for our show comes from the listeners of WBEZ Chicago. WBEZ management oversight for our program by Mr. Torey Malatia, who owns a very, very big radio. This is what he says about it.
<v Ira Glass>要购买此节目或我们任何节目的磁带，请致电我们在芝加哥的WBEZ电台，电话312-832-3380，或访问我们的网站，您也可以在那里免费收听我们的节目，thisamericanlife.org。本节目来自芝加哥市，市长理查德·M·戴利。《美国生活》由公共广播国际部发行。我们节目的资金来自WBEZ芝加哥的听众。WBEZ对我们节目的管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚先生负责，他拥有一台非常、非常大的收音机。这就是他关于它的话。

00:56:59.110 --> 00:57:08.190
<v Griffin Hansbury>This big, shuddering, warm, inanimate object would just drive me crazy. It was very erotic to me.
<v Griffin Hansbury>这个巨大的、颤动的、温暖的、无生命的物体会让我发疯。它对我来说非常色情。

00:57:08.190 --> 00:57:14.210
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。

